(1173, 'App\\Models\\User', 2, 'created', 'App\\Models\\Blog', 10, '[]', '{\"title\":\"Wazir-e-Azam Apna Ghar Programme: How to Apply\",\"slug\":\"wazir-e-azam-apna-ghar-programme-how-to-apply\",\"category\":\"Real Estate\",\"short_content\":\"sd\",\"content\":\"<h2>Wazir-e-Azam Apna Ghar Programme: What You Actually Need to Know<\\/h2><p>Every month the rent goes out, and the same quiet question follows it: will we ever own a place of our own? For a lot of Pakistani families, the honest answer has been \\\"probably not.\\\" The <strong>Wazir-e-Azam Apna Ghar Programme<\\/strong>, also called <strong>\\\"Ghar Ho Tu Apna,\\\"<\\/strong> is the government\'s attempt to change that.<\\/p><h2>The Programme, Explained Simply<\\/h2><p>Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif launched it on 30 April 2026. The plan is to finance 500,000 homes over five years at a cost of Rs3.2 trillion. In year one, the target is 50,000 houses, with Rs321 billion set aside.<\\/p><p>It isn\'t a giveaway. It\'s a subsidised loan. You borrow from a participating bank, and the government covers part of the markup in the early years.<\\/p><h2>The Numbers, in Simple Terms<\\/h2><table><tr><th>Feature<\\/th><th>Details<\\/th><\\/tr><tr><td>Loan Size<\\/td><td>Up to Rs. 10 million<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Markup Rate<\\/td><td>5% fixed for the first 10 years<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Loan Tenure<\\/td><td>Up to 20 years<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Down Payment<\\/td><td>10% of the property value<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Bank Financing<\\/td><td>Up to 90% of the property value<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Processing Fees<\\/td><td>No processing charges<\\/td><\\/tr><tr><td>Early Repayment<\\/td><td>No penalty for early repayment<\\/td><\\/tr><\\/table><p>After year ten, the rate moves to market level. It is around one-year KIBOR plus 3%, so your installment can climb. Plan for that now rather than being surprised later.<\\/p><p>During the subsidised period, estimated monthly installments are:<\\/p><listing><li>Rs25 Lakh loan works out to roughly Rs16,500 a month.<\\/li><li>Rs50 Lakh is about Rs33,000 a month.<\\/li><li>Rs75 Lakh is about Rs49,500 a month.<\\/li><li>Rs1 Crore is about Rs66,000 a month.<\\/li><\\/listing><p>These are estimates. Your bank will give you the exact figure.<\\/p><h2>What Can You Buy or Build?<\\/h2><p>You can buy a house or flat, buy a plot and build on it, or build on land you already own. There are size limits: a house up to 10 marla (2,720 sq. ft.) or a flat up to 1,500 sq. ft. It\'s for living in, so rental and investment properties don\'t count.<\\/p><h2>Who Qualifies?<\\/h2><p>The basic eligibility requirements are simple:<\\/p><ul><li>You need a valid CNIC.<\\/li><li>You must be a first-time homeowner.<\\/li><li>You can\'t have a housing unit in your name.<\\/li><li>You shouldn\'t have already used another housing scheme.<\\/li><li>Salaried people, business owners, freelancers and informal earners can all apply.<\\/li><\\/ul><p>The finer points vary a little from bank to bank:<\\/p><ul><li>Age between 25 and 60, with the loan maturing before 60 (salaried) or 65 (self-employed).<\\/li><li>Minimum income of Rs25,000 a month for you and Rs20,000 for a co-applicant.<\\/li><li>At least 2 years of employment if salaried, or 3 years of business proof if self-employed.<\\/li><li>Debt burden ratio under 33% of your net monthly income.<\\/li><li>A clean ECIB credit record.<\\/li><\\/ul><p>Check your ECIB history before you apply. It\'s better to find a forgotten old dues problem yourself than to hear about it from the bank.<\\/p><h2>How to Apply<\\/h2><p>There are two routes:<\\/p><ul><li>You can go through the online portal at apnaghar.gov.pk.<\\/li><li>The process is also bank-driven, and you can simply visit any participating commercial bank, Islamic bank or microfinance bank. Either way, the bank makes the final call.<\\/li><\\/ul><h3>On the Portal<\\/h3><p>On the portal, the application process goes like this:<\\/p><listing><li>Create an account with your name, CNIC, mobile number, email and password.<\\/li><li>Log in and click Apply Now.<\\/li><li>Pick your income category: salaried, business, or informal.<\\/li><li>Choose your bank and branch.<\\/li><li>Select the purpose, enter the amount, and set your equity (you can put in more than 10%).<\\/li><li>Fill in personal details, plus co-applicant and guarantor information if needed.<\\/li><li>Add employment details, using your net income, not gross.<\\/li><li>Upload your documents, review everything, and submit.<\\/li><\\/listing><h3>Documents You Will Be Needing<\\/h3><p>Keep these documents ready:<\\/p><ul><li>CNIC (front and back).<\\/li><li>A recent photograph.<\\/li><li>Employment certificate and salary slip.<\\/li><li>Six months of bank statements.<\\/li><li>Account maintenance certificate (it\'s separate from the statement).<\\/li><li>Next of kin\'s CNIC.<\\/li><\\/ul><p>Zameen also mentions utility bills and property papers if you already own a plot, and some banks may ask for 6 to 12 months of statements.<\\/p><h2>A Few Things Worth Knowing<\\/h2><ul><li>The bank\'s approved evaluator will value the property, so your own estimate won\'t count.<\\/li><li>Construction loans are released in phases tied to progress.<\\/li><li>Since there\'s no processing fee, anyone asking you for money to \\\"fast-track\\\" your file is a red flag. Pay nothing before approval.<\\/li><li>Freelancers and the self-employed should expect to show stronger paperwork, like bank statements and tax returns. Some people report the process can take a while, so start early.<\\/li><\\/ul>\",\"cover_image\":\"\\/images\\/saima_blog_cover.jpg\",\"faqs\":\"[]\",\"status\":\"publish\",\"is_published\":true,\"author\":\"Saima Editorial\",\"meta_title\":null,\"meta_description\":null,\"meta_keywords\":null,\"id\":10}', 'https://fsadvisory.pk/admin/blogs', '203.101.188.87', 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/157.0.0.0 Safari/537.36', NULL, '2026-11-16 11:30:16', '2026-11-16 11:30:16'),
